{title:Title}

{phang}
{bf:cjoinby} {hline 2} C-accelerated joinby: form all pairwise combinations within groups


{marker syntax}{...}
{title:Syntax}

{p 8 17 2}
{cmdab:cjoinby}
[{varlist}]
{cmd:using}
{it:filename}
[{cmd:,} {it:options}]

{synoptset 28 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{syntab:When observations match}
{synopt:{opt update}}replace missing values in memory with values from {it:filename}{p_end}
{synopt:{opt replace}}also replace nonmissing values in memory with nonmissing values from {it:filename}; requires {opt update}{p_end}
{syntab:When observations do not match}
{synopt:{opt unm:atched(none)}}ignore all; the default{p_end}
{synopt:{opt unm:atched(both)}}include from both datasets{p_end}
{synopt:{opt unm:atched(master)}}include from the data in memory{p_end}
{synopt:{opt unm:atched(using)}}include from the data in {it:filename}{p_end}
{syntab:Variables}
{synopt:{opt _merge(varname)}}{it:varname} marks the source of each observation; default is {cmd:_merge}{p_end}
{synopt:{opt nol:abel}}do not copy value-label definitions from {it:filename}{p_end}
{syntab:Data recovery}
{synopt:{opt snap:shot}}use {help preserve} to restore data after an error or interruption{p_end}
{synopt:{opt fast}}skip data recovery after an error or interruption{p_end}
{syntab:Reporting}
{synopt:{opt verbose}}display detailed timing information{p_end}
{synopt:{opt thr:eads(#)}}maximum number of threads to use{p_end}
{synoptline}

{pstd}
{varlist} may not contain {cmd:strL} variables.


{marker description}{...}
{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:cjoinby} reimplements Stata's {cmd:joinby} ({cmd:joinby.ado} 2.1.0,
December 22, 2022). See {help cjoinby##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for
details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:cjoinby} joins, within the groups formed by {varlist}, each observation
of the dataset in memory (the master data) with each observation of
{it:filename} (the using data), forming all pairwise combinations. The result
replaces the data in memory. {it:filename} is a Stata-format dataset; the
extension {cmd:.dta} is assumed when none is given.

{pstd}
If {varlist} is not specified, {cmd:cjoinby} joins on the variables common to
both datasets and names them. Missing values of the key variables form groups
like any other value, and each extended missing value is a value of its own.

{pstd}
Observations that only one of the datasets has are left out unless
{opt unmatched()} says otherwise. Variables that both datasets have, other
than the key variables, keep the master's values unless {opt update} is
specified.


{marker options}{...}
{title:Options}

{dlgtab:When observations match}

{phang}
{opt update} changes what happens to the variables, other than the key
variables, that both datasets have. By default the master's values stand. With
{opt update}, a missing value in the master data takes the value from the using
data.

{phang}
{opt replace}, allowed with {opt update} only, also replaces nonmissing values
in the master data with the values from the using data. A nonmissing value is
never replaced with a missing value.

{dlgtab:When observations do not match}

{phang}
{opt unmatched(none|both|master|using)} specifies whether observations found
in only one of the datasets are kept. The default is {cmd:unmatched(none)}:
they are ignored. {cmd:unmatched(master)} keeps the observations of the master
data that have no match, {cmd:unmatched(using)} those of the using data, and
{cmd:unmatched(both)} both. The words may be abbreviated.

{dlgtab:Variables}

{phang}
{opt _merge(varname)} names the variable that marks the source of each
observation: 1 for an observation from the master data only, 2 from the using
data only, and 3 from both. The default name is {cmd:_merge}. As in
{cmd:joinby}, the variable is created only when {opt unmatched()} or
{opt _merge()} is specified.

{phang}
{opt nolabel} prevents copying the value-label definitions of {it:filename}.
Without it they are copied, and a definition the master data already have
under the same name stands. As in {cmd:joinby}, the value labels attached to
the key variables of {it:filename} are copied even with {opt nolabel}.

{dlgtab:Data recovery}

{phang}
{opt snapshot} uses {help preserve} to copy the whole master dataset for
recovery after an error or interruption. Stata may write the copy to disk when
it exceeds {cmd:c(max_preservemem)}.

{phang}
{opt fast} skips recovery to reduce memory use. An error or interruption can
leave a partially joined dataset. It cannot be combined with {opt snapshot}.

{dlgtab:Reporting}

{phang}
{opt verbose} displays the time spent reading the using data, indexing its
keys, matching the master's keys, and writing the joined data.

{phang}
{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256. The
default follows the {help ctools##threads:ctools thread setting}.


{marker remarks}{...}
{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
{bf:Order of the observations.} Each master observation is followed by its
pairings, in the order of the observations in {it:filename}; the master
observations keep their order. Observations kept from the using data only
come after them, in the order of the key variables. When the master data are
sorted, the result is sorted the same way, as {cmd:joinby} leaves it: the
using-only observations are then placed by their values of the sort variables
(they hold their key values, and missing values in the master's other
variables), after the master observations they tie with. {cmd:joinby} leaves
observations that tie in an order that depends on {help set sortseed};
{cmd:cjoinby}'s order does not vary from run to run.

{pstd}
{bf:Variables.} The master's variables come first, then the {cmd:_merge}
variable if there is one, then the variables that only {it:filename} has, with
their formats, labels, notes, and characteristics. The key variables and the
other variables that both datasets have take the wider of the two storage
types; a {cmd:long}/{cmd:float} pair becomes {cmd:double}. They also gain the
notes of {it:filename}'s variables and the characteristics the master's
variables lack, and with {opt update} the variable label and value label of
{it:filename}'s variable where the master's has none. A variable that is a
string in one dataset and a number in the other, and is not a key, keeps the
master's values whatever the options. Under {opt unmatched(using)} and
{opt unmatched(both)}, the using-only observations hold missing values in the
variables both datasets have unless {opt update} is specified; this, too, is
{cmd:joinby}'s behavior.

{pstd}
{bf:strL variables} are supported everywhere but in {varlist}: the plugin
carries the observation numbers of each pairing and the values are copied in
afterwards.

{pstd}
{bf:Differences from joinby.} The order of observations within ties is
fixed, as described above. Without {varlist}, the common variables are found
by their exact names. An empty {it:filename} (no observations) gives the
result of a join without matches, where {cmd:joinby} stops with an error. A
key variable that is a frame alias in the master data is an error (r(106));
use {help frunalias} first. {cmd:e(sample)} stays with the first pairing of
each master observation, where {cmd:joinby} leaves it with one of them.
{cmd:cjoinby} stores results in {cmd:r()}.

{marker failure}{...}
{pstd}
By default, an error or interruption restores the master data. Recovery uses
an in-memory copy of the observations the join moves on Stata 16 or later,
and {help preserve} on earlier versions. {opt snapshot} selects
{cmd:preserve} on every version. {opt fast} disables recovery. The inputs and
the result are limited to 2,147,483,647 observations.


{marker examples}{...}
{title:Examples}

{pstd}Children and their parents, one observation per pairing:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse child, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cjoinby family_id using https://www.stata-press.com/data/r19/parent}{p_end}

{pstd}Keep the observations without a match as well, and mark the source:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse child, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cjoinby family_id using https://www.stata-press.com/data/r19/parent, unmatched(both)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. tabulate _merge}{p_end}

{pstd}Join on two keys, filling in missing values of the other variables both
datasets have from the using data:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cjoinby firm year using deals.dta, update}{p_end}

{pstd}Join on every variable the datasets have in common:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cjoinby using deals.dta}{p_end}

{pstd}Report where the time goes, using at most four threads:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cjoinby firm using deals.dta, verbose threads(4)}{p_end}


{marker results}{...}
{title:Stored results}

{pstd}
{cmd:cjoinby} stores the following in {cmd:r()}:

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 20 24 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(N)}}number of observations in the result{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(N_1)}}number of observations from the master data only{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(N_2)}}number of observations from the using data only{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(N_3)}}number of pairings{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(time)}}elapsed time in seconds with {opt verbose}; missing otherwise{p_end}

{p2col 5 20 24 2: Macros}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(using)}}name of the using file{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(keyvars)}}key variable names{p_end}


